-      <p class="why-it-matters">A fair game has zero expected gain at every step, and a martingale is its formal model. A player may try to beat the game by choosing when to quit. The doubling strategy is the classic attempt: keep doubling the stake after each loss until a win. This lesson states the optional stopping theorem, which says the expected final value of a martingale equals its start whenever the stopping time is bounded. It proves the theorem, applies it to the doubling strategy with finite capital, and shows that the no-capital version breaks it. The conclusion depends on a boundedness hypothesis that the proof uses.</p>
